      "text": "To determine the value of \\( n \\) that makes the seven-digit number \\( 854n526 \\) divisible by \\( 11 \\), we can use the divisibilityrule for \\( 11 \\). According to this rule, a number is divisible by \\( 11 \\) if the difference between the sum of the digits in the odd positions and the sum of the digits in the even positions is a multiple of \\( 11 \\) (including zero).\n\nLet's identify the digits of the number \\( 854n526 \\):\n- Digits in the odd positions: \\( 8, 4, 5, 6 \\)\n- Digits in the even positions: \\( 5, n, 2 \\)\n\nFirst, we calculate the sum of the digits in the odd positions:\n\\[\n8 + 4 + 5 + 6 = 23\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the sum of the digits in the even positions, excluding \\( n \\):\n\\[\n5 + 5 + 2 = 12\n\\]\n\nThe digit \\( n \\) in the even positions needs to be added, and then the difference between these two sums should be a multiple of \\( 11 \\). Let's denote the sum of the digits in the even positions including \\( n \\) as \\( S \\):\n\\[\nS = 12 + n\n\\]\n\nThe difference we need to consider is:\n\\[\n23 - (12 + n) = 11 - n\n\\]\n\nFor the number to be divisible by \\( 11 \\), \\( 11 - n \\) must be a multiple of \\( 11 \\). Therefore, we set up the equation:\n\\[\n11 - n = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad 11 - n = 11\n\\]\n\nSolving these equations:\n1. \\( 11 - n = 0 \\)\n   \\[\n   n = 11\n   \\]\n2. \\( 11 - n = 11 \\)\n   \\[\n   n = 0\n   \\]\n\nHowever, \\( n \\) must be a digit (i.e., an integer from \\( 0 \\) to \\( 9 \\)) and cannot be \\( 11 \\). Therefore, the only valid solution is:\n\\[\nn = 0\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( n \\) that makes the number \\( 854n526 \\) divisible by \\( 11 \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]",
